window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"17-35","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"17-35-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-35-01/","title_he":"א – צונן","title_en":"Cold","body_en":"<p><i>Le-khatḥila</i>, one must be careful that permitted foods do not touch forbidden ones, nor meat touch cheese. If they did touch and both were dry and cold, they are permitted without rinsing. If there is a concern that crumbs of one stuck to the other, one should rub the place where they touched in order to remove them. If there was moisture or greasiness at the place where they touched, one must remove everything that stuck there by rinsing, and when necessary also by rubbing. If meat touched soft cheese that cannot be rinsed, one must remove from the cheese and discard everything that touched the meat, and the meat must be rinsed (Ḥullin 107b; SA 91:1; Shakh 1; Pitḥei Teshuva 1).</p><p>Bread that touched moist meat may be eaten with meat. If one wishes to eat it with milk, since the bread cannot be rinsed without becoming repulsive, one must scrape off from the bread whatever touched the meat. Likewise, bread that touched moist cheese, if one wishes to eat it with meat, one must scrape off from it whatever touched the cheese (SA 91:3; Pitḥei Teshuva 4).</p><p>If a piece of meat fell into milk, if they are cold there is no absorption, and one must take out the piece of meat and rinse it well, and it is permitted (SA 91:4). The milk is also permitted, because even if a little moisture from the meat mixed into the milk, it is nullified in sixty.</p><p>Some are stringent and hold that all this applies on condition that the piece of meat was raw, but if the cold meat was cooked or roasted, it may absorb milk in its outer layer, and therefore one must remove from it <i>kedei kelipa</i> (the thickness of a peel), and the rest is permitted. And if the cooked or roasted meat has cracks, or it is seasoned with a sharp spice, even though it is cold, the milk may be absorbed throughout it, and as long as this does not involve a great loss, all of it is forbidden (Rema 91:7).<sup>1</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"If meat touched hard cheese and one of them was moist, one must rinse the meat and the cheese, and if one did not rinse them and cooked them, the food is kosher (Taz 91:1, and see Pri Megadim there). The SA 91:2 writes that it is permitted to place kosher and non-kosher food together when one usually rinses the kosher food, for example raw meat with non-kosher meat. End quote. It seems that they ruled so because of the pressure of storage conditions, but <i>le-khatḥila</i> one should not place them together.<br>In Ḥullin 112a it is explained that the raw meat of a young pigeon that fell into dairy gravy is kosher (the Rishonim explained that it must be rinsed), but roasted meat requires peeling. And if it has cracks or is seasoned, all of it is forbidden. End quote. Some say that the Gemara's intent in saying that roasted meat requires peeling is that it is hot. The same law applies to cooked meat, and the same law applies to beef. And if the hot meat has cracks or is seasoned, all of it is forbidden. But if the meat is cold, even if it is roasted, cracked and seasoned, rinsing suffices (Rashi, Rashba, Ran, Ritva, Meiri and SA 91:7).<br>On the other hand, some say that even when the roasted or cooked meat is cold, it must be peeled, because it has softened somewhat and can absorb milk in its outer layer. And if it has cracks or is seasoned, all of it is forbidden. But if it was not cooked or roasted, rinsing suffices (Semag, Semak, Or Zarua, Issur Ve-hetter He-arokh). Some are stringent according to this approach even with raw meat, that if it has cracks or is seasoned, all of it is forbidden (Terumah, Sha'arei Dura, Pri Ḥadash and the Gra). On the other hand, some say that according to this approach the stringency applies only to poultry, since it is soft by nature (Tosafot, Rosh and Pri To'ar 91:15). The main view within this approach is that the prohibition applies to cold roasted or cooked meat, and there is no difference between poultry and beef (Terumah, Sha'arei Dura, Rema and Shakh). So the Rema 91:7 wrote in practice, and in a case of great loss he ruled like the SA."}]},{"slug":"17-35-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-35-02/","title_he":"ב – בישול בכלי ראשון","title_en":"Cooking in a Keli Rishon","body_en":"<p>Cooking takes place in a <i>keli rishon</i> (primary vessel), that is, in a pot standing on a fire or another source of heat. The power of cooking, through heat and liquids, is that it mixes the flavors of foods into one another. Therefore if, for example, one cooked non-kosher meat with legumes, their flavors mix into one another, and therefore if the permitted food does not contain sixty times the forbidden food, the entire mixture is forbidden (above 34:3-4).</p><p>Even after the vessel has been removed from the fire, or the fire under it has gone out, since it was heated on the fire, it is still considered a <i>keli rishon</i> that can cook. This is on condition that its heat is at least <i>yad soledet bo</i> (the heat from which the hand recoils) (SA 105:2; 45° Celsius, see Peninei Halakha, Shabbat 10:4). But if its heat dropped below this temperature, it does not cook, and the law of foods placed into it is like the law of cold foods that fell into one another (as explained in halakha 1; the law of pickled food will be explained in halakha 9).</p><p>And although often a <i>keli rishon</i> that is not on the fire does not have the power to cook, to expel the flavor in the forbidden piece and to cause it to be absorbed into the permitted foods, since it sometimes does have the power to cook, the Sages established a rule: as long as there is no reliable gentile who tasted and said that there is no taste of the forbidden food in the permitted foods, one must regard everything in the <i>keli rishon</i> as if it had been fully cooked. And if the permitted food does not contain sixty times the forbidden piece, then even after the forbidden piece is taken out, the dish is forbidden (this will be explained further below in halakha 5; see also above 34:4).<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"Some say that if a forbidden piece fell into a <i>keli rishon</i> and was immediately taken out of the dish, the dish does not become forbidden, since the taste of the forbidden food did not have time to come out (Pitḥei Teshuva 105:8, based on Ḥamudei Daniel; Nefesh Ḥaya YD 40; and similarly wrote the Radbaz 1:223). So wrote the Arukh Ha-shulḥan 105:43, and in his opinion, if the dish has no gravy, one may be lenient and take out the forbidden food even after several minutes, and the dish is permitted. On the other hand, according to many poskim, even if the forbidden food was taken out of the dish immediately, we calculate as if all its flavor mixed into the dish (Pri To'ar 98:9; Yad Yehuda 105, long commentary 12; Tzur Ya'akov 1:188; Pri Megadim, Mishbetzot Zahav 68:9:1; Rav Pe'alim vol. 1 YD 20, and so is implied by several Rishonim). This is because we do not know how to estimate how long it takes for the flavor to come out of the forbidden piece and mix into the rest of the dish, and therefore, as long as there is no one who can tell us with certainty that there is no forbidden taste there, one must be stringent according to the rules.<br><b>A <i>keli rishon</i> that is not on the fire</b>: The essential cooking is in a <i>keli rishon</i> that is on the fire; nevertheless, even when it is not on the fire, according to most poskim it cooks by Torah law. So wrote the Rashba, Rashbatz, Pri Ḥadash 68:18, Pri Megadim, Mishbetzot Zahav 9, Erekh Ha-shulḥan OḤ 318:11, and others. Some wrote that a <i>keli rishon</i> that is not on the fire cooks only by rabbinic law, in accordance with the words of the Yerushalmi (Shabbat 5:3; and so wrote Ramban).<br><b>A <i>keli rishon</i> that is not <i>yad soledet bo</i></b>: Some are concerned that it causes absorption throughout (Yam Shel Shlomo), and some are concerned for <i>kedei kelipa</i> (Shakh 105:5). The opinion of the overwhelming majority of poskim is that its law is like cold (SA 105:2, Taz and others)."}]},{"slug":"17-35-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-35-03/","title_he":"ג – עירוי מכלי ראשון","title_en":"Irui (Pouring) from a Keli Rishon","body_en":"<p>If one poured what was cooked in a <i>keli rishon</i> onto other foods, the <i>irui</i> (pouring) has the power to cook them <i>kedei kelipa</i>. For example, if one poured boiling non-kosher gravy onto meat, the surface of the meat becomes forbidden and must be peeled off, and the rest of the meat must be rinsed well and is permitted to eat. <i>Kedei kelipa</i> is the thinnest layer of the food that can be peeled off in one piece.</p><p>If one poured hot water from a <i>keli rishon</i> onto a kosher piece of meat and a piece of <i>nevela</i>, flavor can pass from the <i>nevela</i> into the kosher piece <i>kedei kelipa</i>, because <i>irui</i> can expel and cause absorption simultaneously. Therefore, one must remove the outer layer of the kosher meat and discard it, and rinse the rest well (SA 68:10).<sup>3</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"Regarding the law of <i>irui</i>, there are, in general, three opinions among the Rishonim: a) According to Rabbeinu Tam, <i>irui</i> cooks completely. b) According to the Terumah and those who follow him, it cooks <i>kedei kelipa</i>. c) According to the Rashbam and those who follow him, it does not cook <i>kedei kelipa</i>, but it causes flavors to be absorbed and expelled <i>kedei kelipa</i>. Let us detail the three opinions in halakha and practice:<br>a) According to Rabbeinu Tam, the Ra'avya and the Yere'im, <i>irui</i> cooks completely. Some hold that according to Rabbeinu Tam <i>irui</i> cooks only <i>kedei kelipa</i> (Rosh, Tur and Ritva), but according to most Rishonim, in Rabbeinu Tam's view <i>irui</i> cooks everything (Ramban, Rashba, Mordekhai, Rabbeinu Yeruḥam, Perisha 68:8, Pri Ḥadash 18, and others). The halakha does not follow him.<br>b) According to the Terumah, Semag, Rosh, Rabbi Yeḥiel of Paris, Mordekhai and Tashbetz Katan, <i>irui</i> cooks <i>kedei kelipa</i>. So it was ruled as halakha in the SA and Rema 68:10; 105:3. So wrote many other Aḥaronim as well, among them: Shakh, Levush, Knesset Ha-gedola, Ḥokhmat Adam, Zivḥei Tzedek. Some poskim follow the Terumah but are stringent, holding that in addition to <i>irui</i> cooking <i>kedei kelipa</i>, it causes flavors to be absorbed throughout the whole dish (Yam Shel Shlomo, Baḥ).<br>c) According to the Rashbam, Ra'avad, Ramban, Meiri, Ra'ah and Ran, <i>irui</i> causes absorption and expulsion <i>kedei kelipa</i> but does not cook (and therefore it does not involve the Torah prohibition of cooking meat and milk, nor the Torah prohibition of cooking on Shabbat). (According to the Ittur, it does not even cause absorption and expulsion <i>kedei kelipa</i>.)<br>In practice, when boiling meat fell into cold milk, or boiling milk fell onto cold meat, several poskim ruled that <i>be-di'avad</i> sixty times its outer layer is not required (Rema 91:4; Shakh 8, Pri Ḥadash 8, and many others). The main explanation for this is that in their opinion, the Terumah and those who follow him, who said that <i>irui</i> cooks <i>kedei kelipa</i>, said so as a stringency, but <i>be-di'avad</i> it can be regarded as not cooking (Magen Avraham 467:33; Derisha YD 91:3; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 16-17) (and thus it turns out that <i>be-di'avad</i> the opinion of the Rashbam is taken into account). One could further say that in their opinion <i>irui</i> indeed cooks <i>kedei kelipa</i>, but that ordinarily one is stringent in defining the thickness of the peel as a layer that can actually be peeled, whereas <i>be-di'avad</i> one can regard <i>kedei kelipa</i> as extremely thin, and consequently it is nullified in sixty.<br><b><i>Irui</i> whose stream was interrupted</b> is <i>irui</i> that did not create a connection between the <i>keli rishon</i> and the food it struck; rather, before the poured liquid struck the food, it had already separated from the vessel from which it came, and since it moved away from and was cut off from its source, its power weakened. Some say its law is like a <i>keli sheni</i>, which does not cook and does not cause absorption and expulsion (Rema 68:10; Levush 10; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 32). And some say that it causes absorption or expulsion, but not absorption and expulsion simultaneously, and therefore if one poured boiling water onto a kosher piece and a non-kosher piece, non-kosher flavor does not enter the kosher piece (Shakh 105:5; Knesset Ha-gedola, Pri Megadim 68, Mishbetzot Zahav 9; Ḥokhmat Adam, Zivḥei Tzedek 91:21)."}]},{"slug":"17-35-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-35-04/","title_he":"ד – כלי שני, שלישי וגוש","title_en":"Keli Sheni, Keli Shelishi and Gush","body_en":"<p>A <i>keli sheni</i> (secondary vessel) is the vessel into which a dish was poured from the vessel that stood on the fire, and since its walls were not heated on the fire like a <i>keli rishon</i>, the hot dish in it does not have the power to cook. And even though it is still scalding, since the walls of the vessel cool it, it does not have the power to cook. However, <i>le-khatḥila</i> we take into account the opinion of those who hold that it has the power to expel and cause absorption of flavors <i>kedei kelipa</i>, and if, for example, a zucchini fell into non-kosher soup in a <i>keli sheni</i>, one peels the zucchini and the rest is kosher (SA 105:2; above halakha 1).</p><p>A <i>keli shelishi</i> (tertiary vessel) is the vessel into which a dish was poured from the <i>keli sheni</i>, and since it is removed by another degree from the source of heat, its power to mix flavors is weakened, and even if it is still very hot, there is no concern that it will cause absorption and expulsion <i>kedei kelipa</i> in foods placed in it. Likewise, there is no concern that <i>irui</i> from a <i>keli sheni</i> causes flavors to be absorbed and expelled; rather, its law is like cold.<sup>4</sup></p><p><b><i>Gush</i></b> (a solid mass of food): Some say that everything we have learned, that a <i>keli sheni</i> does not cook, applies only to a dish that is not solid, such as a liquid dish that comes into full contact with the cold walls of the vessel and cools down, or a dish that crumbles, and the air that enters it cools it. But a solid food, such as a piece of meat, a pashtida (casserole) or a potato, retains its heat within it, and even though it was transferred from a <i>keli rishon</i> to a <i>keli sheni</i> or <i>shelishi</i> or beyond, it is not so affected by the walls, and as long as its heat is <i>yad soledet bo</i>, it has the power to cook, or at least to cause flavor to be absorbed throughout the food it touched. And some say there is no difference between solid and liquid, and that the heat of a solid food too is preserved only by virtue of its being in the vessel in which it was cooked, and from the moment it was transferred to another vessel its heat steadily weakens and it is unable to cook or to cause flavors to be absorbed. <i>Le-khatḥila</i> one should act in accordance with the stringent opinion, and in a situation of great loss, in accordance with the lenient opinion (Ḥokhmat Adam 60:12).<sup>5</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"<b><i>Keli sheni</i></b>: Shabbat 40b: “A <i>keli sheni</i> does not cook.” Some say that it does not even cause flavors to be absorbed (Rabbeinu Peretz, Sha'arei Dura, Issur Ve-hetter He-arokh, Maharil). Some say that it causes absorption and expulsion <i>kedei kelipa</i> (Rashba and Tur, as explained in the Beit Yosef; Shakh 105:5). And some say that it causes absorption and expulsion throughout, so that if a zucchini fell into non-kosher soup, all of it becomes forbidden; but it does not cause absorption and expulsion at the same time, so that if a piece of non-kosher meat and a zucchini fell into a <i>keli sheni</i>, only the liquids in the <i>keli sheni</i> become forbidden, and not the zucchini (Yam Shel Shlomo; Taz 105:4, based on the Rishonim who stated without qualification that a <i>keli sheni</i> causes absorption and expulsion). In practice, it was ruled in the SA and Rema 105:2-3 that it is proper to take into account the opinion that it causes absorption and expulsion <i>kedei kelipa</i>, but <i>be-di'avad</i> it does not render food forbidden and its law is like cold. So wrote the Arukh Ha-shulḥan 105:19. Many wrote that one may be lenient in this only in a case of loss (Shakh, Ḥokhmat Adam and Zivḥei Tzedek).<br><b><i>Keli shelishi</i></b>: One authority was stringent that as long as the dish is <i>yad soledet bo</i>, even if it is transferred to a tenth vessel, it causes absorption and expulsion <i>kedei kelipa</i> (Pri Ḥadash). Some say that this is the practice with a <i>keli shelishi</i> but not beyond (Ḥatam Sofer). Most poskim hold that the law of a <i>keli shelishi</i> is like cold (Pitḥei Teshuva 94:7; Tuv Ta'am Va-da'at; Sha'ar Ha-tziyun 451:10). The same law applies to <i>irui</i> from a <i>keli sheni</i> (Magen Avraham 447:9; Erekh Ha-shulḥan 451:5).<br><b><i>Yad nikhveit</i></b> (heat at which the hand is scalded): Some are stringent and hold that everything we have learned so far concerns a situation of heat that is <i>yad soledet bo</i>, but at a heat at which the hand is scalded, one must be stringent even with a <i>keli sheni</i> (Ḥokhmat Adam 59:6). Simply, it seems that he would be stringent also with a <i>keli shelishi</i> and beyond. However, the accepted practice is to rule like the rest of the poskim, who were not stringent regarding <i>yad nikhveit</i> even with a <i>keli sheni</i> (Darkei Teshuva 105:56; Badei Ha-shulḥan 35; Shevet Ha-levi 9:70).<br>Although the laws of cooking on Shabbat and of kashrut are similar, there are differences between them. On Shabbat, cooking is defined as preparing a food so that it becomes fit to eat, and therefore on Shabbat one is stringent regarding <i>kalei ha-bishul</i> (foods that cook easily) even with <i>irui</i> from a <i>keli sheni</i> (Peninei Halakha, Shabbat 10:7). However, regarding prohibitions of eating, what matters is the absorption of flavors into one another, and consequently the law of <i>kalei ha-bishul</i> is like that of other foods (Maharil, Ḥamudei Daniel, Yad Yehuda). On the other hand, regarding prohibitions of eating, cooking also means the absorption of flavors without a deep process of cooking, and therefore one is stringent with a <i>keli sheni</i> <i>kedei kelipa</i>. In the prohibition of meat and milk both foundations are present: if actual cooking took place, the prohibition is by Torah law, and if there was only absorption of flavors, the prohibition is rabbinic."},{"n":5,"en":"Those who are stringent regarding <i>gush</i> are: Issur Ve-hetter He-arokh (36:7), Yam Shel Shlomo (Ḥullin 8:71), Torat Ha-asham, Magen Avraham, Zivḥei Tzedek, Rabbi Shlomo Kluger, Ein Yitzḥak. Within this opinion, some say that the law of <i>gush</i> is like a <i>keli rishon</i>, which can even actually cook (Magen Avraham 318:45, Erekh Ha-shulḥan), and many hold that it can cause absorption and expulsion throughout the food but cannot cook (Minḥat Ya'akov, Ḥavot Da'at, Pri Megadim 94, Mishbetzot Zahav 14).<br>On the other hand, some hold that the law of <i>gush</i> is like that of liquid foods, and so wrote the Rema 94:7; 105:3; Levush, Pleti, the Gra, Mishkenot Ya'akov. So too the Ḥatam Sofer YD 95 proved from the words of the Rishonim. However, even in their opinion, as long as the food has not been placed on a vessel, its law is still like a <i>keli rishon</i>.<br>Since the dispute about <i>gush</i> is evenly balanced, <i>le-khatḥila</i> one should be stringent, and in a case of great loss one may be lenient. Similarly wrote the Ḥokhmat Adam 60:12; Yad Yehuda, Arukh Ha-shulḥan 94:32."}]},{"slug":"17-35-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-35-05/","title_he":"ה – ביאור כללי התערובות","title_en":"Explanation of the Rules of Mixtures","body_en":"<p>The rules that the Sages established in the laws of mixtures and absorptions, such as “nullification in sixty” and “a <i>keli rishon</i> cooks,” are fences that the Sages set in order to distance a person from cases in which he would eat food in which a forbidden taste can be detected. But the intention is not to determine that always, or in most cases, the forbidden taste can be detected in accordance with the rules.</p><p>That is to say, although often the forbidden taste is nullified in a quantity forty times greater than it, since in remote cases, when the forbidden taste is strong, it is nullified only in sixty, the Sages determined that all prohibitions are nullified in sixty. Therefore, if a piece of non-kosher meat fell into a dish, although often its taste is nullified in forty, as long as the dish does not contain sixty times the forbidden piece, all of it is forbidden.</p><p>Not only that, but if the dish did not contain sixty times the non-kosher piece that fell into it, even though it was taken out of the dish, the dish remains forbidden, out of concern that perhaps the entire flavor of the non-kosher piece came out into it. Not only that, but if that same non-kosher piece falls again into a second dish that also does not contain sixty times it, that dish too becomes forbidden. That is, we are concerned that perhaps all the flavor came out into the first dish, and that perhaps all the flavor came out into the second dish. And so too with a third dish (SA and Rema 98:4).</p><p>Moreover, the rule does not distinguish between whether the non-kosher piece stayed in the dish for a minute or for an hour (above note 2), because there is no way to determine how much flavor comes out in a minute and how much in an hour. Not only that, but even if the dish was removed from the fire, although its heat weakened, since it sometimes still has the power to cook as when it was on the fire, the Sages established a rule that even a <i>keli rishon</i> that is not on the fire cooks, and therefore if the non-kosher piece falls into it and it does not contain sixty times that piece, all of it becomes forbidden.</p><p>That is to say, the rules set a fence for situations of doubt in order to distance a person from the prohibition, but they do not determine that this is the actual situation. Therefore, if a reliable gentile tastes the dish and says that no forbidden taste can be detected in it, the doubt is resolved and there is no longer any need to use the rules of the fence, and the mixture is permitted even though it does not contain sixty times the forbidden food (above 34:4). But when it is not possible to ask a reliable gentile, one must act according to the safety rules. And since the gentile's answer has sometimes been found to be inaccurate, the custom is not to rely on a gentile, except in cases of great loss (above 34:5).</p><p>It also seems right to explain that all the disputes among the Rishonim and Aḥaronim in the laws of mixtures and absorptions, such as regarding <i>irui</i> from a <i>keli rishon</i>, a <i>keli sheni</i> and <i>gush</i>, concern the question of how far the fence must be extended in order to prevent a stumbling. The main approach in halakha is that the rules of the fence are intended to fence off even remote cases, but not to be excessively stringent and fence off extremely rare cases. And if in practice someone encounters a rare case in which the prohibition was nullified according to the rules and nevertheless its taste can be detected, he should stop eating. As for what he already ate, he did not transgress a prohibition, since he acted according to the guidance of the Sages and did not intend to eat something forbidden.<sup>6</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"Seemingly, one may ask about all the disputes in the laws of mixtures and absorptions in the Talmud, the Rishonim and the Aḥaronim: these are disputes about reality, so why did they not examine the actual reality and determine the halakha accordingly? However, it is difficult to determine what the reality is, because the laws depend on taste, which is elusive and hard to define. The more the forbidden food is mixed into a larger quantity, the more its taste becomes diluted until it fades away, and when the taste is faint, people with a fine sense of taste detect it while most people do not. And some people are sensitive to certain tastes and do not detect others. Likewise, foods change from season to season and from place to place, so that the same kind of food sometimes releases more flavor and sometimes less. Therefore, it is not possible to estimate precisely the quantity of forbidden food that can impart flavor, and the Sages had to establish rules that are fences serving as a safeguard against the Torah prohibition. And since these rules are intended to fence off even remote cases, often in practice the prohibition is nullified in less than the rule sets, but as long as no reliable gentile has checked this, we must follow the safety rules as explained above (see Noda Bi-Yehuda, Mahadura Kamma, YD 28).<br>It also seems right to explain that since the rules are intended to place a fence before the prohibition, the basis of the disputes about the parameters of the rules is the question of how far the fence must be extended. As we find in the Talmud (Ḥullin 98b), that there was a dispute even about the rule of nullification in sixty, and some say that since in rare cases the forbidden food imparts flavor up to a hundred times, prohibitions are nullified only in a hundred (the verses cited are an <i>asmakhta</i> [scriptural support], as explained by the Rishonim). In practice, it was ruled that prohibitions are nullified in sixty (ibid. 97b; SA 98:1). Thus, according to halakha, the rules are intended to set up a fence against remote cases, but not against extremely rare cases. Similarly, the Rishonim and Aḥaronim disagreed about <i>irui</i>, a <i>keli sheni</i> and <i>yad nikhveit</i>, and in all the disputes most poskim follow the approach that the rules must fence off remote cases but not extremely rare ones. And sometimes the disputes are evenly balanced, since the case under discussion is not extremely remote, as with <i>gush</i>.<br>A further foundation: the rules are defined and not subject to estimation. Therefore, if a non-kosher piece fell into one pot and then another, each pot must contain sixty times the piece, lest it released all its flavor into it. True, according to several Rishonim, if the non-kosher piece was taken out of the dish, it is already clear that not all of it was mixed into it, and therefore one must estimate how much flavor it released, and if according to the most stringent estimate it released, for example, half of its quantity, there must be sixty times half of it and not sixty times all of it (Ra'avad, Rid, above 32, note 2). However, the halakha was ruled that even if the non-kosher piece was taken out of the dish, the dish can be permitted only if it contains sixty times the non-kosher piece, for otherwise the rule would be subject to personal estimation, which would vary from person to person, whereas the main aim in establishing the rule is to remove doubts."}]},{"slug":"17-35-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-35-06/","title_he":"ו – צלייה","title_en":"Roasting","body_en":"<p>Two pieces of meat, one kosher and the other non-kosher, were roasted or baked together on the fire while touching each other, whether they were one on top of the other or one beside the other. If one of them was fatty, that is, it contained fat, then by means of the fat the forbidden taste spreads and penetrates the entire permitted piece, and therefore if it does not contain sixty times the forbidden piece, all of it is forbidden. If both were lean, since there is no fat there to spread throughout the piece, one must remove from the permitted piece <i>kedei netila</i>, that is, the width of a finger (1.9 cm), and the rest is kosher (SA 105:4-5).</p><p>Since in practice it is often difficult to define the condition of the fat, the custom is to be stringent according to both possibilities: if the permitted piece does not contain sixty times the forbidden food, all of it is forbidden, lest one of the pieces is fatty; and if the permitted piece does contain sixty times the forbidden food, one must remove <i>kedei netila</i> from it, lest both are lean. All this applies to kinds of meat, where there is almost always doubt about their fattiness, but with other foods, when it is entirely clear that both pieces are lean, one need only remove <i>kedei netila</i> from the kosher piece (Rema 105:5; Zivḥei Tzedek 43).<sup>7</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":7,"en":"It is explained in Ḥullin 96b that when forbidden and permitted foods were roasted together, if the forbidden food is fatty, such as a kid roasted with its <i>ḥelev</i> (forbidden fat), “it is forbidden to eat even from the tip of its ear,” that is, since the <i>ḥelev</i> of the kid is fatty, it spreads throughout the meat until even the ear of the kid becomes forbidden because of it. On the other hand, if both were lean, such as when a thigh was roasted with the <i>gid ha-nasheh</i> (sciatic nerve), “he peels and eats until he reaches the sinew.” The same law applies if meat was roasted with the <i>ḥelev</i> of a lean kid. Thus there are two laws, fatty and lean, and the Rishonim disagreed about both of them:<br><b>Fatty</b>: If one of the pieces was fatty, whether it was the permitted one or the forbidden one, the forbidden taste spreads throughout the permitted food, even to the kid's ear. Some say that if the permitted food contained sixty times the forbidden food, the entire permitted piece remains permitted (Tosafot, Rambam, Ramban and others). And some say that even if the kid's meat contains sixty times the <i>ḥelev</i>, all of it is forbidden, because the fattiness of the <i>ḥelev</i> does not spread evenly, and regarding every spot there is doubt whether perhaps it is present there in a proportion greater than one sixtieth (Ra'ah and Ritva). The SA 105:5 ruled like the Rashba, that although in general the permitted piece is permitted, one must remove <i>kedei netila</i> (1.9 cm) from the place where the permitted piece touched the forbidden one, out of concern that the forbidden taste is concentrated there more.<br>According to many, even if the forbidden piece is lean, when the permitted food is fatty, the forbidden taste spreads throughout the permitted food (Eshkol, Tur and SA 105:5). And some say that only if the forbidden food is fatty does its taste spread throughout the permitted food, but if the forbidden food is lean and the permitted food fatty, only <i>kedei netila</i> becomes forbidden (Rashba, Pri Ḥadash and the Gra).<br><b>Lean</b>: The Gemara's wording regarding the meat of a lean kid that was cooked with its <i>gid ha-nasheh</i> is: “he peels and eats until he reaches the sinew.” Some say that with a lean piece, the entire permitted piece remains permitted, and one need only peel off from it the forbidden food that touched it (Rashi and Rambam). Some say that <i>kedei kelipa</i> of the permitted food becomes forbidden because of its contact with the forbidden food (Roke'aḥ and Meiri). And according to most poskim, one must peel <i>kedei netila</i> from the permitted food (Tosafot, Eshkol, Rosh, Ritva and SA 105:4).<br>Several Rishonim wrote (Hagahot Maimoniyot, Rabbeinu Peretz and Maharil) that since we are not expert in defining what is lean, doubt often arises whether the <i>ḥelev</i> that was roasted with the meat is fatty or lean, and one must be stringent according to both possibilities: perhaps it is fatty, and therefore if the permitted food does not contain sixty times the forbidden food, everything is forbidden; and perhaps it is lean, and therefore even if the permitted food contains sixty times the forbidden food, one must remove <i>kedei netila</i> from it. So ruled the Rema 105:5. The Ḥokhmat Adam 60:7 wrote that with any forbidden and permitted foods that were roasted together, since both pieces are hot, one must be concerned that perhaps they contain fattiness. On the other hand, the Pri To'ar 105:10 wrote that one need be stringent only regarding <i>ḥelev</i>, but if both pieces are of lean meat, there is no need to be concerned that one of them is fatty. I wrote above in accordance with the opinion of the Zivḥei Tzedek 105:43, who decided that with meat one must always be concerned that perhaps it is fatty, while with other foods, if it is clear that they are lean, one may be lenient and remove only <i>kedei netila</i>. In his opinion, even with meat, in a case of great loss or on Erev Shabbat, if it is clear that both pieces are lean, one may permit it."}]},{"slug":"17-35-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-35-07/","title_he":"ז – תתאה גבר","title_en":"Tata'a Gavar – The Lower One Prevails","body_en":"<p>We have learned so far that with cold foods there is no absorption, whereas with cooking in a <i>keli rishon</i> there is complete absorption. The question is what the law is when a cold food and a hot food were placed one on the other. In practice it was ruled: <i>tata'a gavar</i>, that is, the lower one prevails, because the nature of heat is to rise upward. Therefore, if there was hot soup in a <i>keli rishon</i>, and cold <i>tereifa</i> meat fell into it, since the lower one prevails, we are concerned that complete cooking took place there, and if the soup does not contain sixty times the non-kosher piece, it becomes forbidden. If the kosher soup was cold and hot <i>tereifa</i> meat fell into it, since the lower one prevails, we are not concerned about complete cooking, but we are concerned about absorption to the extent of <i>kedei kelipa</i>, and therefore it suffices for the soup to contain sixty times the outer layer of the <i>tereifa</i> meat (Pesaḥim 76a, following Shmuel; SA 105:3).</p><p>Similarly, if two pieces of meat were placed one on top of the other, one kosher and one <i>tereifa</i>: if the lower one is hot at the level of a <i>keli rishon</i>, even if they are not on the fire, the severity of the contact between them is like the severity of the cooking of fatty roasted meat (as explained in the previous halakha), and consequently, if the kosher piece does not contain sixty times more than the forbidden one, all of it has become forbidden. If the lower one is cold, the power of the lower one prevails and the contact between them does not have the severity of cooking; however, since the upper piece is hot, there is absorption there <i>kedei kelipa</i>, and one must peel the kosher piece at the place where it touched the <i>tereifa</i>.<sup>8</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"The Amoraim disagreed in Pesaḥim 76a: according to Rav, <i>ila'a gavar</i> (the upper one prevails), and according to Shmuel, <i>tata'a gavar</i> (the lower one prevails). Since the Gemara cited a <i>baraita</i> in accordance with Shmuel's opinion, even though in matters of prohibition the ruling usually follows Rav, here they ruled like Shmuel (SA 91:4; 105:3). It makes no difference whether one of the pieces is salted and fatty or not (Rema 105:11; Pri Megadim 91, Mishbetzot Zahav 5). According to the Yere'im and Ra'avya, the entire discussion and the conclusion that the lower one prevails apply when the two pieces are equal, but if one is noticeably larger than the other, the larger one prevails even if it is the upper one; and so wrote the Arukh Ha-shulḥan 91:11-12. On the other hand, the rest of the Rishonim and Aḥaronim did not write this fundamental distinction, which means that in their opinion the law of <i>tata'a gavar</i> applies even when the upper piece is larger. So wrote Tosafot Rid, Pri Megadim 68, Mishbetzot Zahav 9; Yad Yehuda (105, long commentary 12).<br>Seemingly one may ask: this is a dispute about reality, so why did the Sages not examine the actual reality and determine the halakha accordingly? However, sometimes there is no influence of taste from one piece to the other, and sometimes there is, but sometimes the lower one prevails and sometimes the upper one prevails, and they disagreed about what happens in most cases in order to give a rule by which people should act (see Noda Bi-Yehuda YD Mahadura Kamma 28, and Pri Megadim 91, Mishbetzot Zahav 7). In addition, sometimes the tastes are faint and elusive and hard to ascertain, and therefore they disagreed about their parameters (Pleti 91:2; Sefer Yehoshua, Psakim U-khtavim 578; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 91:9-10)."}]},{"slug":"17-35-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-35-08/","title_he":"ח – מלוח כרותח","title_en":"Salted Is Like Boiling","body_en":"<p>The Sages said: <i>meli'aḥ ke-rote'aḥ</i> (salted is like boiling). Salted food is food that is not eaten on its own because of how salty it is. The rule is that if the salted food touched another food and there is moisture between them, the salted food causes its flavor to be absorbed into the other food, similar to the absorption caused by boiling. This is because the salt causes the food to release moisture and flavor from within it and to cause them to be absorbed into the food it touches. However, the salted food itself does not take in flavor from the <i>tafel</i> (unsalted) piece. Therefore, if a salted piece of meat touched a piece of unsalted cheese, the salt in the meat causes the meat's flavor to go out into the cheese, but the unsalted cheese does not release flavor into the meat, and it suffices to rinse the meat and it is permitted. If the cheese was salted and the meat unsalted, the cheese imparts flavor to the meat, whereas the meat does not impart flavor to the cheese. And if both pieces are salted, both impart flavor to each other (Ḥullin 113a; Pesaḥim 76a).</p><p>And how much of the other piece does the salted piece render forbidden? If both were lean, without fattiness, the salted piece renders the unsalted one forbidden <i>kedei kelipa</i>; and if one of them was fatty, the salted piece renders the entire unsalted piece forbidden, because by means of the fattiness the taste of the salted piece spreads throughout the unsalted one. And if the unsalted piece was sixty times the size of the salted one, it does not become forbidden (SA 91:5-6). And since it is often difficult to determine whether one of them is fatty, the custom is to be stringent according to both possibilities (Rema 91:5; 105:9; above note 7). Therefore, if the piece that received flavor from the salted one contained sixty times it, its outer layer must be forbidden, for perhaps they are lean and its outer layer became forbidden; and if it does not contain sixty times it, all of it must be forbidden, because perhaps one of them is fatty.<sup>9</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"Pesaḥim 76a: “Shmuel said: Salted food is like boiling food. We said this only when it cannot be eaten because of its saltiness, but if it can be eaten despite its saltiness – no.” The Rishonim disagreed about the definition of “cannot be eaten because of its saltiness”: a) According to the Ra'avan and Rabbi Ya'akov of Israel [?] (Tosafot Ḥullin 112a, s.v. “<i>hanei milei</i>”), it was salted in a manner akin to curing, in order to preserve the meat for a long time. b) According to Rashi, the Ra'avad and the Ra'avya, it is the way one salts meat in order to store it for a certain time without its spoiling, but not for a long time. c) According to Rabbeinu Tam, the Rosh, the Rashba, Rabbeinu Yeruḥam, the Ran and others, it is like the salting of meat to remove its blood in order to permit it for cooking, which must be to a degree at which it cannot be eaten because of its saltiness (above 22:6), and so it was ruled in the SA 91:5. The Rema is concerned that perhaps we are not expert in this, and therefore he was stringent <i>le-khatḥila</i> to regard salting for roasting as salted. The Yad Yehuda (long commentary 20) explained that this refers to abundant salting for roasting, and not to ordinary salting for roasting.<br>There are three opinions regarding the length of time after which salting makes a piece “salted”: <b>a)</b> According to the Ra'avan and Ran, only after the piece has remained in salt for the time it takes to walk a <i>mil</i> (which is the length of time of salting meat to remove its blood; see SA 69:6) does the salt cause it to release all its moisture, and it is considered salted. So is implied by the SA 91:5, and so wrote the Zivḥei Tzedek 27. <b>b)</b> According to the Rashba and Rosh, already a few minutes after salting, the piece begins to release moisture, and from then on it is considered salted for as long as it continues to release moisture, even if the time of walking a <i>mil</i> has passed. This is also the opinion of the Shakh 11, the Pri Ḥadash 9, and many others. Some explain that this is also the opinion of the SA (Yad Yehuda, Arukh Ha-shulḥan 91:30). <b>c)</b> Some say that the piece is considered salted only from the time it began to release moisture until the end of the time of walking a <i>mil</i>, and the Terumat Ha-deshen 159 and the Rema 91:5 wrote that in a case of great need one may rely on them. This is also the opinion of the Shakh in a case where meat was kashered of its blood by salting: at the end of the time of walking a <i>mil</i> the power of the salt is exhausted, and the piece is no longer considered salted and boiling. So ruled the Kreti, the Pri Megadim, the Ḥavot Da'at and others.<br>There are four opinions regarding how far the salted food causes absorption: a) According to the Rambam (Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Assurot 15:34), Ramban and Semag – throughout. b) According to the Ra'ah – <i>kedei netila</i>. c) According to the Ra'avya – <i>kedei kelipa</i>. According to all three of these opinions, there is no difference between a lean and a fatty piece. d) According to the Rashba, Rosh, Rabbeinu Yeruḥam, Eshkol, Or Zarua, Tur and SA (91:5; 105:9), if the piece is lean, <i>kedei kelipa</i>, and if fatty, throughout. However, the Rema wrote (ibid.) that since it is difficult to distinguish between a fatty piece and a lean one, the custom is to be concerned with every piece that perhaps it is fatty, except for completely lean foods (see above note 7).<br>According to the SA 105:11, the salted food renders the unsalted one forbidden on condition that it is below, since the lower one prevails. According to the Rema and many Aḥaronim, even when the forbidden food is above, it renders the unsalted one forbidden."}]},{"slug":"17-35-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-35-09/","title_he":"ט – כבוש כמבושל","title_en":"Pickled Is Like Cooked","body_en":"<p>The Sages said that <i>kavush</i> (pickled, i.e. steeped) is considered like cooked; that is, when foods are steeped in liquids continuously for a full twenty-four hours, all the flavors of the foods in the liquids are absorbed into one another as in cooking. Therefore, if a kosher food and a non-kosher food were soaked in liquids for twenty-four hours, and there was not sixty times the non-kosher food, everything is forbidden.</p><p>If the steeping took place in very salty or sharp liquids, the steeping can take effect already within the amount of time it takes to bring them to a boil on a fire (SA 105:1). Many estimated this time at about 18 minutes. If the time of steeping was less than this, one must remove <i>kedei kelipa</i> from the kosher food (ibid.).</p><p>The poskim disagreed as to whether a vessel too absorbs flavor through steeping, for example, if non-kosher meat soup was soaked in a pot for twenty-four hours, whether the pot must be kashered. The halakha was ruled that <i>le-khatḥila</i> one should be stringent and kasher it by <i>hagala</i>, but if food has already been cooked in it, the food does not become forbidden.<sup>10</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"Ḥullin 111b: “Shmuel said: Pickled food is like cooked food.” Some explain that only food steeped in a sharp liquid or one as salty as brine causes absorption like cooking, but food steeped in water and other liquids that are not sharp or salty does not cause absorption like cooking (Rashi, Rashba, and many in explaining the Rambam's opinion). According to most poskim, food steeped in any liquid is considered cooked (Rabbeinu Tam, Tosafot, Ra'avad, Or Zarua, Rosh, Rabbeinu Yeruḥam, Ritva, SA YD 105:1). Some say that the duration of this steeping is three days (Ra'avya, Maharam and Yere'im), and according to most poskim it is twenty-four hours (Rosh, Rabbeinu Yeruḥam, Sha'arei Dura, SA ibid., and others). Regarding a sharp liquid, some say that steeping in a sharp liquid too causes absorption in twenty-four hours (Issur Ve-hetter He-arokh, Shakh and Taz), and some say that it causes absorption within the time needed to bring to a boil (SA 105:1, Magen Avraham, Binat Adam and others). The Aḥaronim were uncertain how many minutes the time of bringing to a boil is: 10, 18 or 24 minutes.<br><b>Steeping in vessels</b>: Some hold that steeped food causes flavors to be absorbed in a vessel as well, as cooking does (Issur Ve-hetter He-arokh, Shakh and Pri Megadim). Some hold that it causes absorption only in the outer layer of the vessel (Taz). And some hold that it causes absorption in wooden and earthenware vessels but not in metal vessels (Pleti, Erekh Ha-shulḥan). The custom is to rule like the stringent opinion, and therefore one must kasher vessels in which a forbidden food was steeped. However, since according to the basic law the halakha follows the lenient opinion regarding glass and metal vessels, one who wishes to make do with rinsing them may do so (so they ruled even before the discussion about the metal vessels of our times, above 32:6-9).<br>However, the law regarding foods that were cooked or steeped in vessels in which a forbidden food was steeped is more lenient. First, if they are metal or glass vessels, according to the overwhelming majority of poskim, food cooked or steeped in them does not become forbidden (Issur Ve-hetter He-arokh 2:8; Erekh Ha-shulḥan 105:1; Pleti 1, Yad Yehuda, and many others, and unlike the Taz, who is stringent <i>kedei kelipa</i> with all vessels). And even if the vessels are of earthenware or wood, some say that foods cooked or steeped in them are permitted, since the requirement to kasher them is a stringency (Ḥikrei Lev vol. 1 YD 77; Yabi'a Omer vol. 2 YD 6). Some say that foods cooked in them became forbidden, but foods steeped in them are permitted, since by the time twenty-four hours of steeping pass, the forbidden taste in them has already become spoiled (Taz 105:1, Shakh 2; Knesset Ha-gedola). Some say that foods steeped in them are also forbidden, on condition that they were steeped immediately after the absorption of the forbidden food (Magen Avraham 447:16; Pri Ḥadash YD 105:1). And some say that as long as the steeping of the foods began within twenty-four hours from when the pot became forbidden through steeping, the foods steeped in it become forbidden, because the flavor absorbed in the vessel does not become spoiled while it is soaking in the steeping liquids (Pleti 1).<br>In practice, even if one cooked food in a vessel in which a forbidden food was steeped, the food is kosher. If it is a metal or glass vessel, this is agreed upon by almost all the poskim. And even if it is an earthenware vessel, it may be permitted on the basis of a <i>sfek sfek sfeika</i> (triple doubt): a) some say that steeping applies only to sharp liquids; b) some say that steeping does not apply to vessels; c) some say that even if steeping applies to vessels, the foods cooked in them did not become forbidden (Ḥikrei Lev), and all the more so if they were steeped in them."}]},{"slug":"17-35-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-35-10/","title_he":"י – ריח וזיעה","title_en":"Smell and Vapor","body_en":"<p>The Amoraim disagreed about the law of a kosher food that took in the smell of a forbidden food, for example, if a cake and fatty <i>nevela</i> meat were baked in one oven, and the smell of the <i>nevela</i> meat entered the cake, to the point that when eating the cake one senses the smell of the meat but does not detect its taste. In practice it was ruled that <i>le-khatḥila</i> one must be careful that the smell of a forbidden food not enter foods, but <i>be-di'avad</i> the smell does not render it forbidden, and the cake is kosher to eat.<sup>11</sup></p><p>But if a great deal of vapor of a forbidden food entered a kosher food, for example if both were being cooked or baked in an oven and one cover completely covered both of them, since the vapor under the cover is very abundant, the vapor that comes out of the forbidden food renders the permitted food forbidden (Rema 108:1). Likewise, if one cooked non-kosher soup, and instead of covering the pot with a lid, covered it with zucchinis, the zucchinis that absorbed a great deal of vapor are forbidden (Rema 93:1).<sup>12</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":11,"en":"The Amoraim disagreed on a matter of principle: whether <i>reiḥa milta</i> (“smell is something”), that is, whether smell is considered something significant that renders food forbidden like taste. According to Rav it is forbidden, and according to Levi it is permitted (Pesaḥim 76b). According to Rabbeinu Ḥananel, Rabbeinu Tam, the Ra'zah, the Ra'avya and the Ra'avan, the halakha follows Rav, that smell renders food forbidden. According to most Rishonim, the halakha follows Levi, that smell does not render food forbidden, among them: Rav Hai Gaon, Behag, Rif, Rashi, Rambam, Ramban, Rashba, Ra'ah and others. Among those who rule like Levi, some hold that only <i>be-di'avad</i>, if the smell of the forbidden food was absorbed in the food, does it not render it forbidden, but <i>le-khatḥila</i> one must be careful about this (Behag, Rif, Ra'avad and Meiri), and some hold that even <i>le-khatḥila</i> it is permitted for the smell of the forbidden food to be absorbed in the food (Ramban, Ra'ah, Ritva and Ran).<br>In practice, it was ruled in the SA 108:1 that <i>le-khatḥila</i> one must avoid a situation in which the smell of a forbidden food will be absorbed in a kosher food, and <i>be-di'avad</i> the food is kosher. There are many discussions as to when there is a concern that the smell of the forbidden food will enter the permitted food, as explained in the SA and Rema there."},{"n":12,"en":"The Rosh wrote (Responsa, Kelal 20:26) that if one cooked milk and above it was a pot of meat, and vapor rose from the milk to the meat, it renders it forbidden, because the law of the vapor of a food is like the law of the food itself. The Terumat Ha-deshen 2:103 explained that this is on condition that the vapor was hot and so abundant that it heated the meat to <i>yad soledet bo</i>. So wrote the SA and Rema 92:8. The Baḥ (Responsa Ha-ḥadashot 24) added a condition, that the vapor has no room to disperse, but if it has room to disperse, the meat does not become forbidden. So wrote the Arukh Ha-shulḥan 92:55. Several Aḥaronim explained that the entire prohibition can apply only to liquids, whose vapor is abundant, but vapor rising from foods is not so abundant in quantity that it can heat to such a degree and render food forbidden (Pri Megadim, Pitḥei Teshuva 92:6). The opinion of the Mishkenot Ya'akov YD 34 is that even if the vapor heated the food to <i>yad soledet bo</i>, it does not render it forbidden, since it has no taste, and the reason a pot lid was forbidden is that it absorbs actual substance from the dish. See above 25, note 15, regarding baking meat and milk in an oven one after the other."}]}]});
